4.3.2 - The announced tournaments on the clan site.
  - Besides those starting within four days, the Tournaments window sent to the site carries the
    tournaments the game announces ahead (show_before_announce: Trials of Olympus, Season's Legacy,
    Dark Omens...) and the clan tournaments (Ragnarök), up to 120 days ahead, with their start.
  - 'tbcollector live-tournaments' also writes the game's whole schedule (route 24001) as it came.

4.3.1 - The application's icon.
  - tbcollector.exe, tbcollector-config.exe, the configuration window and the installer show the
    treasure chest logo (assets/icon.ico, made from assets/logo.png).

4.3.0 - The game's Tournaments window on the clan site.
  - After the tournament search, the running tournaments and those starting within four days are read
    over the network (routes 24001, 24201, 24003 and 38003; the rating service at rating-proxy) and
    sent to the site with the clan's points and place, the best clans, the clan's players and the
    rewards of each place (Table 44 of the static data). The site lists them, active then inactive.
  - The game's own images (banners, icons, item icons) are read from its Unity CDN (UnityFS, LZ4,
    RGBA32 -> PNG) and sent only when the site lacks them or the game changed them.
  - The best clans are named by their own realm's server: the realms the game's page asks are
    remembered (clan_directory.json), and a request to another realm no longer changes the session.
  - Days to look back defaults to 45. 'tbcollector live-tournaments' writes the window to a file.

4.2.0 - The clan's results in the tournaments, and publishing decided by the clan site.
  - The tournament search reads the Journal card with the clans' ranking of each tournament: the
    clan's place and points among the clans listed and what the clan received (the Trials of
    Olympus and the Dark Omens list it; the others by the reward tiers of the place). The items are
    named from the game's catalogue (Table 8; Table 12 for the rewards of a tier). They are sent to
    the site (POST /clan-results) before the rankings; a site with none yet gets the last 45 days.
  - The 'Close complete rankings on the site' setting is gone: each tournament of the site's
    catalogue says whether its rankings are published without review. The collector still says
    when every player has a name and the end date is known; nothing else is published.
  - 'tbcollector journal-dump' writes the Journal's tournament cards and the clan's results, to
    learn a new card's shape. It reads only.
  - A tournament publishing on its own is held for review when a clan prize chosen to be split did not
    come with it, or the clan's result has not arrived; the site tells its administrators.
  - Tournament rankings client version 1.2.0.

4.1.0 - A summary at the end of every log, and the schedule set from the interface.
  - Every run ends its log with a RUN SUMMARY: the status (success, failures, cancelled, not started,
    stopped by an error, nothing to do), the chests collected in total and per profile, each module's
    outcome on each profile with the reason of a failure, what was not due, and every warning and
    error of the run repeated in one place. It is written however the run ends.
  - The Execution tab creates, updates and removes the 'TB Chest Collector' task in Windows Task
    Scheduler, with the interval chosen (5 minutes to once a day) and an optional start time, shows
    how it is scheduled, warns when it starts another folder's tbcollector.exe, and can start it now.

4.0.0 - Rewritten in Rust: two executables, no Python, and a Windows installer.
  - tbcollector.exe is what Windows Task Scheduler calls (it replaces run.bat and main.py, with the
    same options: --check, --gui, --account, --profile, --module, --force, --keep-open, and the same
    exit codes). tbcollector-config.exe is the configuration window (it replaces Configure.bat).
  - The installer (TBChestCollector-Setup-<version>.exe) lets the folder be chosen, adds Start menu
    shortcuts and can schedule an hourly run. Installing into the folder of an earlier version keeps
    its config\config.json, module_state.json and module_data\ as they are: the formats did not change.
  - Every module, the runner, the schedule, the login, the profile switch, the network link to the game
    (requests and the Journal's socket), the chest and tournament catalogues from the CDN, the database
    and the clan site client were ported as they were. The tournaments already sent keep their
    signatures, so nothing is sent twice after the upgrade.
  - tools/fix_chest_types.py and tools/audit_chest_types.py are 'tbcollector fix-chest-types' and
    'tbcollector audit-chest-types'. Check_Environment.bat is 'tbcollector diagnose', which also tries
    every profile's database and clan site.
  - Removed: install.bat, the venv, the portable WinPython and requirements.txt; nothing has to be
    installed besides Chrome. Removed what nothing called: the EventUploader import, JournalRepository
    and insert_error.
  - The database connection uses TLS when the server offers it, as mysql-connector did.
  - A new tab is opened with PUT /json/new, which newer Chrome versions require.
  - Tests: 163 unit tests ported from the Python suite, plus checks run by hand against the real CDN
    (the catalogues decode as the Python version decoded them) and against a real Chrome talking to a
    simulated game (cargo test --test cdp_game -- --ignored).

Next  - Nothing reads the screen any more, and the calibration is gone with what was left of it.
  - A new tournament type goes to the site's catalogue without a picture. The walk no longer tries to
    picture it twice, nor asks the game for the top of the Journal to check that the places held.
  - The incomplete chests no longer carry a picture: the 'screenshot' argument and the query of the
    types with a picture in review are gone (database/repositories.py).
  - The interface opens again: gui/__init__.py still imported the deleted calibration wizard.
  - Removed 'run.bat calibrate', which called a --calibrate main.py no longer has.
  - Removed opencv-python, numpy and Pillow. The screenshot on error is the PNG Chrome renders,
    written as it comes; with them went the capture, the display scaling, the viewport resizing
    and the wheel scrolling, which only the calibrated screens used (core/browser.py).
  - Removed the leftovers in config/ (calibration.json, calib_refs/ and their .bak copies),
    capture.png, and every mention of the calibration in the README, the scripts and the code.

Next  - The Triumphal Gifts tab is collected again. Since 3.0 its gifts (kind 2 in the game's list) were left
    in the game. They are now written and opened over the network like the Gifts tab, and a new type of that
    tab is pictured on it ('Triumphal gifts tab' step, back in the calibration as optional).
  - Opening them this way was first tried on a real run: if the game refuses one or leaves it as it was, what
    was written for it is taken back, the tab is left in the game until the next run, and the run reports it.
    The Gifts tab goes on either way.

Next  - New module, Shield of Peace: asks the game over the network when the city's shield ends and, with less
    than a day left ('Renew with less than', 24 hours by default), buys one with gold and activates it. Off until
    turned on per profile in Accounts and profiles > Modules.

Next  - Accounts work at the same time, each in a Chrome window of its own; the profiles of an account stay one
    after the other. 'Accounts at the same time' (Execution, 4 by default, 1 for the old order).
  - Each account has its own debugging port and Chrome folder, written down the first time and shown in
    Accounts and profiles, where they can be changed. Ports start at 'First debugging port' (9322), which
    keeps them away from other tools on 9222. New folders are in %USERPROFILE%/.total_battle_counter_profiles,
    so each account needs one login by hand.
  - A window is only used or closed after its command line shows it is on the account's folder.
  - A folder that never logged in stops only its account, without typing the password, and 'Open window' is
    where the person logs in. A session that drops later is logged back into as before.
  - Two active profiles on the same database are refused, when saving and when running.
  - Removed 'Clear session between accounts', which would throw the verified session away.

Next  - The OCR is gone, with RapidOCR and Tesseract and their settings. A new tournament type goes to the site's
    Pending Tournaments with a picture of the Journal page, and its rankings wait for someone to name it
    there (needs the site's version with this, and its migration).

Next  - A new chest type is no longer read with the OCR. Its chests go to the site's review queue with a
    picture of the panel, and naming one there names the type (needs the site's version with this).

Next  - Profiles are switched over the network, inside the game's tab, so the tokens never reach Python.
  - The game's list of profiles gives the target's token and id. A switch only counts when the page has
    loaded and the game's requests carry that id, and a name that matches none or several is refused
    (core/session.py). A run no longer needs the profile menu calibrated.
  - The Journal's socket is looked for three times, since it may still be opening after a switch.
  - Removed from the OCR what nothing calls: identify, read_text, read_rows_positioned, card_age_days.
  - Removed the switch through the screen: the three calibration steps of the profile menu, find_text and
    its name matching, the settings Delay after click, Profile switch wait and Name match threshold.

Next  - The store is only watched for when something is about to be read or clicked on screen: before the
    profile menu and before a reading of a panel. A run that stays on one profile and meets no new type
    no longer waits the 15 s for it.

Next  - The clan's members are asked over the network; the clan window is no longer opened.
  - Route 15000 with record 58 gives the membership and route 402 the profiles, 100 at a time
    (modules/members/module.py). The steps 'Clan: Members' and 'Clan: members list' and the settings for
    the wait and the scrolling are gone.

Next  - Which profile the game is on is asked over the network; the banner is no longer read.
  - Every request of the game carries the id of the profile playing, and route 402 gives its name
    (core/session.py). About 2 s, attaching included, against 3 s of OCR, and only the whole name counts, so 'Crash BR' cannot
    pass for 'Cash BR'. The calibration step 'Active profile name' is gone.

Next  - The tournament rankings are read over the network; the Journal is no longer walked by clicking.
  - The list and each ranking are asked on the game's own Journal socket, without a click and without
    marking any card as read (modules/events/feed.py, modules/events/mapper.py). 3199 entries, the whole
    Journal since 22/11/2024, took 36 s. The players' names are asked for by id (route 402).
  - Which cards are the clan's comes from the list: a classic result carries 1 for the clan's ranking and
    0 for the player's own world ranking (up to 1.6 MB), which is not asked for.
  - The game does not send a tournament's name. A type the site's catalogue does not know waits until
    the end; then the Journal is paged to its first card and the page read. A page that disagrees with
    the list is refused, and those rankings wait for the next search (modules/events/screen.py).
  - Removed: 'Reload the game before searching', its wait, restarting Chrome before the rankings
    (execution.fresh_browser_for_rankings), and the calibration of the steps that opened a card
    (open card, Show details, ranking list, close ranking, close card). The Journal's calibration is
    no longer required to run: without it, known tournaments are read and new ones wait.

Next  - The clan chests are collected over the network; the screen is no longer involved.
  - The game's list and the opening of each gift are requests sent from inside the game's tab, with the
    session learned from the game's own requests, kept in memory and never logged or saved. Nothing is
    clicked, captured or read for the chests (modules/chests/game_link.py, modules/chests/from_game.py).
  - One request at a time, each waiting for its answer, at least 'Time between chests' apart (0.2 s). The
    game answers in about 0.27 s, so 3 to 4 gifts per second.
  - A player the site does not know is named by the game. A type is named by game_chest_types or by the
    chests already written with it, when they all agree.
  - A type nothing names waits until the known chests are done. Then the panel is opened, scrolled to the
    first of them and read; the type is registered and collected over the network, and so on for the
    next one. A reading that does not match the list leaves the chests for the next run, unless they are
    about to expire (modules/chests/screen.py).
  - Removed: the 'Test chest capture' button, the chest click delay and known-names settings,
    database/vocabulary.py and tools/chest_traffic.py. A database too old for the game's ids now fails
    with a message; it used to read the screen.

Next  - One run at a time, and a reused Chrome is checked for the flags that keep it drawing.
  - On 19/10 the scheduled run started at 18:51 while a module run from the interface was still in the
    Journal. Both drove the same Chrome, the newcomer reset the game under the other, and every profile
    failed after that. A run now holds config/run.lock (an OS lock, dropped when the process ends,
    however it ends); one that finds it held logs a warning and does not start (exit code 0)
    (utils/run_lock.py, core/runner.py, main.py).
  - From 17/10 the Journal's "Show details" was almost never found (similarity stuck at 0.46): the
    ranking did not arrive, closing the card closed the whole Journal and the profile failed. It worked
    again on other runs with nothing in the collector changed. The suspect is Chrome throttling an
    unseen window: the collector had been reusing a Chrome it had not opened, so the anti-throttling
    flags were never checked. A reused Chrome whose command line lacks any of them is now restarted with
    them (core/browser.py).
  - When "Show details" is not recognised, the log says whether Chrome thinks the page is visible and
    focused, and the screen is saved once per run as <time>_events_show_details_not_found.png. The
    failure screenshot came after the Journal was closed and showed only the city (modules/events/mapper.py).
  - A run with the tournament rankings due starts by closing the browser and opening a fresh one, so the
    Journal is read on a freshly loaded game rather than on a Chrome left open for hours. Those runs
    happen a few times a day; chest-only runs keep reusing the open browser. New setting in Execution:
    'Restart browser before tournament rankings' (on by default) (core/runner.py, modules/base_module.py).

Next  - A run starts with the profile the game opened on.
  - The game always comes up on the profile used last. The run followed the configured order all the
    same: opened on the second profile, it switched to the first and later back to the second - two
    trips through the profile menu and two loading waits to end up where it started.
  - Now the banner is read once, right after the game loads and the shop is closed. If it names a
    profile with something due, that one is collected first, with no switch, and the others follow in
    their order. A banner that cannot be read, or a profile with nothing due, leaves the order as
    configured (core/runner.py, core/session.py).

Next  - The clan chests are read from the game's own list, not from the screen.
  - When the clan window is opened the game sends the list of gifts waiting (route 15000): for each
    one its id, the id of the player who sent it, the chest type, when it expires and how many chests
    it is (an epic monster drops up to six at once). Gifts generated during the run are announced one
    at a time (event 300020), and the game answers once for every chest opened (route 15013). The
    collector now writes each chest from that - player by id, type by id, generation time to the
    second - and uses the screen only to find the 'Open' buttons (modules/chests/gifts.py,
    modules/chests/from_game.py).
  - Why: measured on a real run of 859 chests, every chest was in the list, in the order the panel
    shows them, and the screen got one batch of four wrong - the panel was captured before it redrew,
    so a chest was written twice and another never written. No reading of the screen can catch that;
    the list does. It also ends names read wrong and members a reading cannot tell apart
    ('Alok BR' / 'ALOK BR').
  - A tab ends by asking the game for the list again and making the database agree with it: a gift
    opened and not written is written, one written and still waiting is taken back.
  - The game sends a chest's type as a number. The site's new game_chest_types table names each one
    (chest name and source, linked to the standard_chests row of that source); a type met for the
    first time is named from the screen, once. A gift is written whole and carries its game ids
    (game_gift_id, game_type_id, game_player_id in collected_chests), so a run that stopped halfway
    can be repeated without writing a chest twice.
  - Needs the clan site with the migrations CreateGameChestTypes and AddGameIdentityToChests. On an
    older site, and for the Triumphal Gifts tab (empty on every recording made, so how the game lists
    those is not known yet), the chests are read from the screen as before. New settings in the
    module: 'Read the chests from the game's own list' (on by default) and 'Wait for the game's list'.
  - Fixed in the game's codec: the length of each event on the tail of an answer counts its own four
    bytes. Read the other way, only the first event of each answer was decoded right.
  - tools/chest_traffic.py records a collection's traffic beside what the screen read and compares
    the two, chest by chest - the measurement all of this was built on.

Next  - A frozen game in Chrome no longer sinks every run after it.
  - The game sometimes freezes in the browser until F5. The run failed, left that Chrome open,
    and every later run found it, attached to the same frozen tab and failed again, in a loop.
  - A browser found already open is now checked before it is used (the tab must run JavaScript
    and render a frame); one that does not answer is closed - even though this run did not
    open it - and launched fresh.
  - When no profile of an account can be collected, the browser is closed, reopened and the
    account tried once more. New setting 'execution.restart_browser_on_failure' (on by default).

Next  - Optional GPU acceleration for RapidOCR, and a stale-package fix.
  - New 'ocr.use_gpu' setting runs RapidOCR's detection and recognition models on the graphics
    card through DirectML instead of the CPU. It works with any DirectX 12 GPU (NVIDIA/AMD/Intel)
    on Windows 10+, with no CUDA/cuDNN version to match - only 'onnxruntime-directml' in place of
    plain 'onnxruntime' (see requirements.txt). Off by default: measured on an RTX 4050 with the
    current PP-OCRv6 models, 403 ms per panel on 4 CPU threads dropped to 103 ms on the GPU, same
    text read back both ways, but the payoff depends on the GPU actually in the machine - an older
    or integrated one can come out slower for a model this small. tools/benchmark_ocr.py measures
    a given machine before the setting is turned on there.
  - Found while investigating a collection-speed report: this machine's Python had
    'rapidocr-onnxruntime' 1.4.4 installed - the OLD package (capped there, older PP-OCRv4 models) -
    instead of the 'rapidocr>=3.0.0' requirements.txt asks for. Both work, but the project's OCR
    tuning (thread key, TINY/hybrid notes in core/ocr.py) was measured against the new package, so
    a machine still on the old one is not running what the code was tuned for. utils/check_env.py
    now says which package is active and how to fix it; the OCR speed complaint itself traced to
    the RapidOCR-first engine swap of 2.0.5 (necessary for the accuracy it bought - see that entry)
    rather than to this, but it is worth fixing on any machine that has drifted onto the old package.

Next  - Run heartbeat for the site's monitoring:
  - Every run writes running/success/partial/failed/cancelled to the site's job_runs table in each
    database it collects into (database/job_runs.py); older sites without the table are skipped.
  - Fixed: a profile that failed an attempt and succeeded on the retry was counted as a failure,
    making the run exit with code 1.

2.0.6 - OCR diagnostics and fallback improvements:
  - Added clear visibility and alerts in the calibration wizard and runner preflight when RapidOCR is unavailable.
  - Improved Tesseract fallback: wide horizontal gap splitting prevents adjacent columns from merging into broken single lines.
  - Added environment verification tool (Check_Environment.bat) to diagnose Python, RapidOCR, ONNXRuntime, VC++ Redistributable, and Tesseract.

0.1   - Initial release.
0.2   - Multiple accounts support.
1.5.0 - Modular redesign (Core, Database, Config, Modules) and OCR-based account switching.
1.5.1 - Faster collection (~6s -> under 0.5s per chest) and account list scrolling fixes.
2.0.0 - Browser-based rewrite: new OCR engine, calibration wizard, 4-at-a-time collection.
  - Browser (CDP): the game runs in Chrome driven by the DevTools Protocol; the desktop app
    and all window/pyautogui code are gone, and clicks now use page coordinates.
  - Accounts and profiles: each account keeps its own credentials, cities and Chrome profile,
    so its verified session survives between runs and no login is needed on every run.
  - New OCR engine: Tesseract first, RapidOCR only on demand. Measured on the real chest
    panel, all at 100% accuracy: Tesseract 310 ms, RapidOCR 462 ms, onnxtr 1040 ms.
  - The database teaches the OCR: members, chest origins, history and manual corrections are
    loaded per profile, and a field recognized there skips the slow engine entirely.
  - 4-at-a-time collection: one capture and one OCR pass per panel of 4 chests, clicked
    bottom-up. 2278 ms -> 212 ms per chest; 1000 chests drop from 38 to 3.5 minutes.
  - Calibration wizard: points, areas and reference images recorded over a game capture, with
    magnifier and per-step viewport. "Test this step" clicks and measures if the screen moved.
  - Configuration UI (run.bat config): every setting, accounts, profiles and databases with
    connection test, calibration and a test run with live log.
  - Unreadable chests become a review queue with the PNG crop, handled in the web interface.
2.0.2 - Translation and localization fixes:
  - Translated complete documentation (README.md), startup/installer batch scripts, and command-line interfaces to English.
  - Full English UI localization: configuration interface, calibration wizard, schema definitions, tooltips, dialogs, and popups.
  - Standardized all application logs, exception messages, cancellation tokens, and database status messages to English.

2.0.5 - OCR engines swapped round, measured on 314 real chest panels.
  - The corpus: every panel saved before its chests were clicked, 3750 lines over 1250 chests,
    the same images then fed to each engine in its own process. The measurement that set the
    old arrangement had used a single panel.
  - RapidOCR now reads and Tesseract is consulted only about words mixing digits with
    digit-shaped letters. Of 257 lines whose spelling was checked against the panel by eye,
    the old arrangement got 204 wrong and this one gets 8.
  - The premise the old arrangement rested on is no longer true: RapidOCR's PP-OCRv6 models
    do carry Latin diacritics. It returned 42 accented lines against Tesseract's 13 and was
    right on every contested one, each of which Tesseract had got wrong: it dropped the cedilla
    from a Turkish surname, turned another Turkish name into a different name entirely, put the
    wrong mark on an o (two different wrong marks, on different panels), lowercased the L in
    VaLi, uppercased the i in EMiRBeY, and read a superscript two as a question mark.
  - The confidence gate is gone. It fired on 14 of 315 reads while 204 lines went out wrong:
    Tesseract is not hesitant about these names, it is confident and wrong. Tesseract also
    spelt three players two ways each, splitting one person's chests across two database rows;
    the new arrangement spelt none of them two ways.
  - Fixed: ocr.threads had been doing nothing since the rapidocr package was renamed. The key
    moved to EngineConfig.onnxruntime.intra_op_num_threads and the library accepts unknown keys
    without raising, so every read had silently run at the package default. Reads went from
    1231 ms to 551 ms per panel. The value is now read back after construction and a warning is
    logged if it did not apply.
  - Speeds per panel after that fix: RapidOCR TINY 131 ms, Tesseract 258 ms, RapidOCR 551 ms,
    the combination 557 ms. A 1200-chest run spends about three minutes inside the engine.
  - Limit worth knowing: the error counts come from the 257 lines the engines disagreed about.
    The other 3493 were read identically by all of them, so a shared mistake would not show up.
    The counts are a floor, not a ceiling.
  - Added: a warning when the text comes back too small to read well. A run collected for
    thirty minutes off a degraded picture - words glued together on more chests than it
    collected, every accent dropped, one player split across six spellings - and nothing in
    the log said anything was wrong; it was only found by reading the database afterwards.
    Two clean runs read the panel at a median 15 page pixels per line (1222 lines, tenth
    percentile 14), so a median under 12 now says so once per run, in the first panel instead
    of in the database three hours later. Measured in page pixels, so the threshold does not
    move with ocr.capture_scale.

2.0.6 - Calibration wizard improvements.
  - The two optional store steps come first. The store is what covers everything else - the
    game opens it by itself - so marking it and its X, and closing it, is now what clears the
    screen before the profile and clan steps rather than being asked for at the end.
  - "Advance automatically" now performs the step. Marking a button and moving on left the
    game a screen behind: the next step asked for something inside a menu whose button had
    just been marked but never pressed, so the whole sequence had to be navigated by hand
    while calibrating. Each step that marks a button now presses it, waits, takes a fresh
    capture and only then opens the next step. If the click changes nothing on screen the
    wizard stays where it is and says so, instead of walking on over the wrong screen.
    Steps that only mark an area to read have nothing to press and just recapture. The
    'Open' button is never pressed automatically: that would consume a chest nothing records.
  - New "Test chest capture" button. The three chest steps only work together - the button is
    found by its picture and each chest's text is placed relative to the button found for it -
    so testing them one at a time proved nothing. It now runs the collector's own detection
    against the live screen and draws the answer: a box per button, a box per text area, and
    what was read in each one, so four chests can be confirmed before a run instead of after.
  - Account fields have labels. A placeholder is not a label: it disappears as soon as
    something is typed, which left a filled-in account as four anonymous boxes. The profile
    rows got column names for the same reason.

2.0.8 - Chests are dated by when the game made them, not by when they were read.
  - `collected_at` now holds the chest's generation time, worked out from the countdown the
    panel prints beside it: a chest is born showing '19 h : 59 m' and falls from there, so
    '12 h : 40 m' left means it was generated 7h19 ago. Before, every chest in a run carried
    the minute the collector happened to reach it - a chest made at 10:51 was stored at 18:11.
    A run takes minutes but the chests in it are up to twenty hours old, and with the game's
    day rolling over at 14:00 that moved whole mornings of chests into the next day's count.
  - Where the countdown cannot be read the row is left for the database to stamp on arrival,
    as before, and the log line says so for that chest. Databases without the column are
    unaffected: the column is asked for once and skipped where it does not exist.
  - The panel's right-hand column no longer leaks into the text. The chest area now spans the
    countdown and the 'Open' button, so the reading came back as 'From: Night King Time left:
    12h:44m' and 'Source: Epic Chimera squad Open' - the button's caption was being stored as
    part of the source. The column is cut off by where things are printed, not by what they
    say: a box carrying part of a countdown, or one starting where the 'Open' button was
    found, begins the column, and whatever is pressed tight against it (the label, the
    hourglass) goes with it. No wording in any language is matched.
  - "Test chest capture" in the calibration wizard reads exactly as the run does and shows
    each chest's countdown and the generation time it would store, so a calibration that
    cannot read the countdown is visible before a run rather than after.
